nit: Consider extending the comment to note the Not(In) safety as well:

```
// SPARK-59605: a NULL in the IN list is inert (`x IN (.., NULL)` matches the same
// rows as IN on the non-null values), and castLiteralValue would NPE on a null
// literal. Drop NULLs; if none remain (e.g. `IN (NULL)`) there is nothing safe to
// push. For Not(In), the pushed NOT(IN(non-nulls)) is over-inclusive (reads extra
// stripes) but safe - Spark re-applies the full predicate after the scan.
```

Consider adding a NOT IN case - it's the subtle one under three-valued logic:

```
// NOT IN with NULL: under three-valued logic, NOT IN (1, 3, NULL) returns
// 0 rows because every non-match evaluates to NULL (not TRUE).
Seq("true", "false").foreach { pushdown =>
  withSQLConf(SQLConf.ORC_FILTER_PUSHDOWN_ENABLED.key -> pushdown) {
    checkAnswer(
      spark.read.orc(path)
        .where("id NOT IN (1, 3, NULL)")
        .select("id", "label"),
      Seq.empty[Row])
  }
}
```

